          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "Blood pressure is written as two numbers, measured in millimeters of mercury (mm Hg). The top number, systolic, is the pressure when the heart contracts. The bottom number, diastolic, is the pressure between beats. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not describe the measuring method itself, such as cuff placement or how the reading is taken.\n\nRepeated readings are needed because blood pressure changes throughout the day. It moves with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep, and time of day. A single high reading in a clinic is therefore not enough to make a diagnosis. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*.)\n\nGuidance says a diagnosis uses the average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions. Readings taken outside the clinic, such as at home or with a 24-hour monitor, help in two ways. They can show \"white-coat\" pattern, where readings are high in the office but normal elsewhere. They can also show \"masked\" pattern, where office readings are normal but readings elsewhere are high. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, major cardiology society consensus summary.)\n\nIf you have questions about your own readings, a clinician can explain what they mean for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
